"""The Defect's strategy on game build v0.111, shared by planning and individual decisions.

Owner's guidance: mixed playstyles and a few archetypes, generally with Frost Orbs; look
for scaling with either Orbs or physical attacks.

The numbers in the text are what the highest-rated Spire Codex players did with the Defect on this
build: 471 solo A10 standard runs of builds v0.111.1 and v0.111.0 by the 20 highest-rated accounts that
have them, 308 won, read 2026-11-25 with ``python -m sts_agent.pilot --build v0.111.0 players --character
DEFECT --players 20 --runs 40 --words Frost,Lightning,Dark,Focus,Claw,Channel`` (the decks carried into
each act boss and what was taken at 8663 card rewards, counted by ``evidence.deck_balance``'s role
rules). The simulated numbers are ``pilot.runs`` on Spire Mentor's v0.111.0 simulator: 120 A10 runs
drafted by its run-policy net, fights searched, no model and no live run. The build names come from the
Spire Codex guides (builds guide and Defect tier list).
"""

PROMPT_VERSION = "defect-strategic-planner-prompt-v0111-1"

DECK_GUIDANCE = """\
THE DEFECT'S DECK. The Defect wins with several builds and usually with a mix of them, so judge the deck by what it does this fight, not by an archetype. What the mix looks like is known. In 471 solo A10 runs on this game version by the highest-rated Spire Codex players (308 won), the deck entering the act 1, 2 and 3 bosses held about 20, 27 and 35 cards, with as many Block cards as damage cards (four, seven and nine of each), 1.4, 2.2 and 2.8 cards that Channel Frost, and one card that applies Weak. It is not kept thin: they took 89% of the card rewards in act 1, 74% in act 2 and 65% in act 3, more often with a large deck than with a small one.

Defense is Frost in nearly every build: a Channeled Frost Orb gains Block at the end of every turn for as long as it stays in a slot, and again when it is Evoked. Those players took a Frost card six times in ten with none in the deck (two times in three among the first three picks), nearly half the time with one, and three times in ten with two to five. The deck_balance track "frost" asks for two and marks Frost over-supplied from six; while it is missing or short a Frost card is the first defensive pick, ahead of a plain Block card of similar size. A Block card was taken half the time with none added, four times in ten up to the fourth and three in ten after, so Block is never marked over-supplied. One card that applies Weak or lowers enemy Strength was taken 44% of the time while the deck had none, 22% with one and 12% with two (the track "weak" asks for one and marks a third as too many). Orb slots are shared: Channeling into full slots Evokes the leftmost Orb, so count the Orbs the deck Channels in a turn against its slots before adding more generators, and do not Evoke away the Frost the defense stands on. The starter's Lightning deals the early damage; it is not a defense. A card that only Channels Lightning was taken one time in six with fewer than two such cards held and one in three from two on, and a first Dark card half the time: a Lightning build is built on once it exists, and one Dark Orb is taken early for the damage it stores.

Scaling comes from Orbs or from attacks, and the deck already leans one way; build that one and take the other only when it costs nothing. Orbs: Focus raises what every Orb does, Frost's Block included; Focus, more slots and Powers that add Block or Orbs every turn were taken four times in ten or more with none to three held, a Power that gives Focus more than eight in ten, so deck_balance counts them as scaling for both damage and Block and marks Block scaling short below four. This route needs Orbs that stay in play, so it wants Frost and slots before Focus. Attacks: an attack that raises its own damage every time it is played, a zero-cost Attack engine with the cards that return or replay them, or lasting Strength; this route wants cheap attacks, draw and removals, and its Orb slots are spare. What those players took less and less is damage cards: one in three with none added, one in five with three and one in eight with six; deck_balance marks damage over-supplied once the deck holds more damage cards than those decks did (from the sixth in act 1, the eighth in act 2, the tenth after). Draw, energy and other utility were taken more often the more the deck held (a quarter of the time with one such card, 38% with six) and in every act, the Powers that replay cards or draw every turn most of all: keep adding them. A Power that needs several turns to pay back is weighed against the act boss's first big attack: it is in play early or it is a skip. Removals: about three a run, more than half of them in act 2, starter Strikes first. Status cards a card adds to the deck are a cost every later draw pays unless something held turns them into value.
"""

ELITE_GUIDANCE = """\
THE DEFECT'S ELITE ROUTES. Follow the act stance below. Elites cost the Defect little more than they cost the Ironclad (in simulated A10 runs on this game version a strong drafter lost about one in eighteen in act 1 and one in eleven in act 2, and gave up a fifth of max HP for an act 1 elite it beat and a quarter for an act 2 one), so route toward them in act 1 and keep taking them later. The highest-rated players do: 2.5 elites in act 1, 2.1 in act 2 and 1.7 in act 3 a run, losing 17%, 18% and 13% of max HP to each, with a deck that by the act 1 boss holds about ten added cards: four that deal damage, four Block cards of which one or two Channel Frost, one or two scaling pieces and one or two draw or utility cards. What differs from the Ironclad is recovery: nothing heals the Defect after a fight (those players enter the act 1 boss at half HP), so place a rest site before or right behind an elite when the map allows, and have Frost or non-starter Block in the deck before an elite with one big attack turn. Say in the plan which elites are accepted and what the deck must pick up before each.
"""
